27:Appeared in the September 18, 2026, print edition as 'One Phone Call Put Sheeran Tour at Risk'.
28:Andrew Beaton is a reporter for The Wall Street Journal in New York covering the NFL, golf and sports. Andrew's coverage has included breaking news about the biggest subjects in football, such as the controversy over player protests during the national anthem, in addition to features about transcendent players and the evolution of the sport. His stories have also documented the battle for the future of golf, which earned him recognition in 2022 from Sabew.
29:Andrew also likes to cover the oddities of the rapidly booming chess world. He first joined the Journal as an intern in 2014 after graduating from Duke University.
30:Katherine Sayre is a music-industry and podcasting reporter in The Wall Street Journal's Los Angeles bureau. Her coverage includes the business of record labels and streaming platforms, the rise of AI in music and the economics of live events. She joined the Journal in 2019 to cover the gambling industry, exploring Las Vegas Strip casinos and the rise of sports betting.
31:Katherine is co-author with former Journal colleague Kirsten Grind of the book “Happy at Any Cost: The Revolutionary Vision and Fatal Quest of Zappos CEO Tony Hsieh.”
32:Before joining the Journal, Katherine was an investigative and business reporter in New Orleans at The Times-Picayune.
33:Elias Leight is a reporter covering music and the music business for The Wall Street Journal. His articles often focus on the new ways artists make and market music in a rapidly evolving industry that has never been more competitive. He also tracks the evolution of popular genres, chart trends and the vinyl comeback.
34:Elias previously spent four years at Rolling Stone and three years at Billboard.
