     3	Macklemore says Patriots' Robert Kraft banned him from stadium over Palestine stance
     4	Robert Kraft, owner of the New England Patriots and their stadium, has successfully had Macklemore removed from pop star Ed Sheeran's concert tour. The Grammy Award-winning rapper made the revelation himself on Monday, Sept. 14, and Kraft later confirmed it.
     5	Macklemore posted to Instagram that Kraft leaned on Sheeran, one of the world's top-selling musicians, to remove Macklemore from his lineup due to the "Free Palestine" position Macklemore expressed earlier this month. Sheeran's "Loop Tour 2026" is scheduled to play at Gillette Stadium, which is owned by Kraft Group, on Sept. 25 and 26 in Foxborough, Massachusetts. Macklemore, who shared his pro-Palestinian sentiments over Labor Day weekend in two separate shows at MetLife Stadium in East Rutherford, New Jersey, just outside New York City, will no longer be invited to perform on the tour.
     6	Per Macklemore, Kraft, who is Jewish, contacted Sheeran and told him "that Kraft said (Macklemore) would not be allowed to perform in his stadium.
     7	“Ed also told me that Kraft had rallied some of the other stadium owners and collectively they gave him an ultimatum: if Macklemore stays on the tour, you will not be allowed to play in our venues.”
     8	Messina Touring Group, which is promoting Sheeran’s show, told Rolling Stone: “As the concert promoter for Ed Sheeran’s U.S. Loop Tour, we have been notified by venues on the upcoming U.S. tour dates that they will not allow a concert to take place with Macklemore on the lineup, which would result in the cancellation of the tour and impact hundreds of thousands of fans. After discussions with stakeholders, Macklemore will not be performing on the remaining support dates.”
     9	Kraft, 85, was concerned Macklemore would spread "hate speech" and "antisemitic rhetoric and imagery" during the tour's New England stop.
    10	"I agree with Macklemore," Kraft expressed in a statement obtained by USA TODAY Sports. "Too many lives have been lost and there has been too much suffering, but only sharing selective information and ignoring the actions of Hamas is not honest and only advances more divisiveness and hate."
    11	Kraft also cited the support he himself has provided to Palestine over the years.
    12	Macklemore, 43, said he and other celebrities involved in the controversy are not victims.
    13	“We have careers, money, opportunities, safety and audiences around the world,” Macklemore also expressed in his post. “Whatever consequences any of us face for what we say, we get to go home to our families.
    14	“The victims are the Palestinian people. The men, women and children who have been killed, starved, displaced and forced to live through unimaginable violence."
    15	Tens of thousands of Palestinians, many of them children, have been killed during Israeli military operations in Gaza during the aftermath of the Oct. 7, 2023, terrorist attack by Hamas, a Palestinian militant group, inside Israel's borders.
    16	USA TODAY Sports has reached out to the NFL seeking comment regarding Macklemore's statements about Kraft but has not heard back from the league.
    17	Macklemore, who characterized Sheeran as a "brother," if one who does not take public political positions, said their 13-year relationship has been strained by recent events and conversations and that "our friendship can't change my responsibility to tell the truth about what happened."
